\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Rephasing two coordinates and changing the eigenbasis}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a complex plane take the Hermitian operators whose
matrices in the initial orthonormal basis are
##{A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}2&\ui\\-\ui&2\end{pmatrix}.}
Both have eigenvalues #{1,3}. Diagonal unitary basis changes
multiply both upper off-diagonal entries by the same unit
phase. Their ratio is imaginary, so these changes alone cannot
make both entries real.}

\p{For the initial vectors #{e_1,e_2}, use instead
#{(e_1+e_2)/\sqrt{2}} and #{\ui(e_1-e_2)/\sqrt{2}}. In this
orthonormal eigenbasis of #{A}, the two matrices are
##{\begin{pmatrix}3&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}.}
This implements \ref{fdip-0191}. Their commutator #{AB-BA} in
the real basis has upper entry #{2}, lower entry #{-2} and
zero diagonal. Its determinant is #{4}, although both
operators preserve the same real form.}
